export const PH6={title:'Planning the community presentation',hook:'Judges score whether your presentation actually educated the public and made a positive impact. That starts long before you speak: choosing the right audience, finding a place and partner, setting objectives and shaping your message for the people in the room. A strong plan turns a school project into real public health work.',objectives:['Choose a target audience and explain why it needs this message.','Find a venue, partner and date for the community presentation.','Write learning objectives and measure impact.','Apply health literacy and plain-language principles.'],sections:[
['Choosing a target audience',[
'The guidelines ask teams to **determine their target audience** and plan **how, when and where** they will present. Good audiences are groups who **encounter health misinformation** and can **act** on what they learn: middle school students starting to use social media, parents in a PTA meeting, older adults at a senior center or library, a youth sports league, a faith community or a community health fair.',
'Learn about your audience: What platforms do they use? Which myths are they likely to see? What is their **health literacy** and preferred language? What do they care about? A presentation for sixth graders might use games and a TikTok-style skit; one for older adults might use large-print handouts and examples from Facebook or forwarded messages.',
'Explain your choice in Round Two. Judges reward teams that show a clear reason for their audience and tailor their message to it.',
match('Audience and tailored approach',[['Middle school students','Interactive games and short skits'],['Parents','Vaccine and children’s health myths with trusted sources'],['Older adults','Large print, forwarded-message examples, supplement myths'],['Athletes','Supplement and recovery myths'],['Spanish-speaking families','Bilingual materials and presenters'],['Health fair visitors','Quick, visual fact-check stations']])]],
['Venue, partners and scheduling',[
'Contact a **community partner**—a library, school, senior center, community center, place of worship, clinic or local health department—and ask to present. Explain the topic and HOSA, propose dates and confirm the room setup, equipment, audience size and length (no more than **10 minutes**, the same as Round Two).',
'Schedule early—ideally by **winter**—so you have time to present, film your trailer and upload by **May 15**. Ask permission to **photograph or record** (for your trailer) and gather consent from audience members who appear on camera.',
'Record the **date and audience** carefully. In Round Two, you must state them before beginning: for example, “The following presentation was completed at Riverside Public Library for 45 parents on March 3, 2027.”',
order('Booking a community presentation',['Choose the target audience','Identify a partner organization','Email a professional request','Confirm date, room, equipment and time limit','Ask permission to record and photograph','Record the date and audience for Round Two'])]],
['Objectives and measuring impact',[
'Write **learning objectives** for your audience. For example: “After the presentation, participants will be able to (1) name the four SIFT steps, (2) identify three red flags of health misinformation and (3) name two trustworthy health information sources.” Objectives keep your content focused.',
'Measure impact with a short **pre- and post-survey** (paper or QR code), a quick **show of hands**, a **pledge** card (“I will check before I share”) or a **follow-up** survey a week later. Collect a few **quotes** or feedback from your partner. Judges score **effectiveness and impact** and reward a closing that includes **impact on the community and feedback**.',
'Keep surveys short and anonymous, and follow your partner’s rules about collecting information.',
sort('Good impact measure?',['Good measure','Weak measure'],[['Pre/post survey: % who can name SIFT steps',0],['“Everyone seemed to like it”',1],['Number of pledge cards signed',0],['Partner feedback quote',0],['Counting how many slides we had',1],['Follow-up survey one week later',0]])]],
['Health literacy and plain language',[
'**Health literacy** is the degree to which people can find, understand and use health information and services. Many adults have difficulty with complex health information, so presentations should use **plain language**: short sentences, common words, active voice and only a few key messages. Define necessary terms (“misinformation means…”) and avoid jargon.',
'Use **visuals**, **examples** and **stories**, and repeat key messages. Check understanding by asking the audience to try a skill, such as fact-checking an invented post. For audiences who speak other languages, provide translated materials and, if possible, bilingual presenters.',
'Plain language is not “dumbing down”—it is making information clear so everyone can use it.',
"Test your materials before the presentation. Ask someone from your target audience—a younger sibling, a parent or a grandparent—to read your handout and explain it back to you. If they cannot, simplify it until they can.",
sort('Plain language or jargon?',['Plain language','Jargon'],[['“Check who wrote it before you share”',0],['“Assess epistemic provenance of content”',1],['“A myth is a false belief many people share”',0],['“Utilize lateral reading heuristics”',1],['“Ask your doctor or nurse”',0]])]],
['Research and accurate sources',[
'Judges score **research and resources**: evidence of **significant and reliable research**. Use the event’s official references—**HHS** resources on health misinformation, the **Johns Hopkins** Center for Health Equity materials on the pandemic of misinformation and **WHO** guidance on combatting misinformation online—plus peer-reviewed studies and reputable health organizations.',
'Keep a source list as you research, and **cite** sources visibly in your presentation (on a poster, a slide or a handout). Practice what you preach: fact-check every statistic and example you use, and make sure examples of myths are clearly labeled as false.',
'Accurate, cited information builds trust with your audience and judges and models the very skills you are teaching.',
chk('Which is the best way to show research in your presentation?',['Cite reputable sources visibly and fact-check every statistic','Use numbers without sources','Rely only on social media posts','Avoid research to save time'],'Research is scored, and modeling good sourcing teaches the topic.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Picking an audience',{a:{text:'Your team considers presenting to classmates, but your local library says its senior group often asks about health posts they see online. (Scenario invented for practice.) Which choice can make a stronger case?',choices:[['The senior group, because they encounter misinformation and asked for help—tailored with large print and forwarded-message examples','b','Correct.',true],['Any audience; it does not matter','b','Judges value a clear, tailored audience.',false]]},b:{text:'How will you show impact?',choices:[['A short pre/post survey on red flags and trustworthy sources, plus pledge cards','c','Correct.',true],['Count how many people clapped','c','Measure learning.',false]]},c:{text:'You book March 3 at the library and record the date and audience for Round Two.',end:true}})]],
["Working with your community partner",["Treat your partner as a collaborator. Ask what their members worry about and which myths they hear most; this makes your presentation more relevant. Share an outline in advance, confirm accessibility needs (seating, microphones, interpreters) and ask about any rules for recording or photographing participants.","On the day, arrive early, set up quietly, introduce yourselves to the host and start and end on time. Afterward, send a thank-you note, share a summary of your survey results and ask for a short quote about the presentation’s impact—useful for your trailer and Round Two closing.","A good partnership can lead to repeat invitations and new audiences, extending the impact of your work well beyond the competition.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Partner communication","steps":["Ask about audience concerns","Share your outline in advance","Confirm room, equipment and access needs","Confirm recording permissions","Present on time","Send thanks, results and request a quote"]}]]
],takeaways:['Choose a target audience that encounters misinformation and can act on it, learn its platforms, myths, language and literacy, and explain the choice; partner with a library, school, senior center or health department.','Schedule early, confirm a 10-minute slot, get recording and photo permission and record the exact date and audience to state before Round Two.','Write learning objectives, measure impact with pre/post surveys, pledges and feedback, use plain language and visuals for health literacy and cite reliable research (HHS, Johns Hopkins, WHO) while fact-checking every statistic.'],terms:[['Target audience','The specific group a presentation is designed for.'],['Health literacy','The ability to find, understand and use health information.'],['Plain language','Clear, simple wording that audiences can easily understand.']],mcqs:[q('What must teams determine for the community presentation?','Their target audience and how, when and where to present','Only the costume colors','The judges’ preferences','Nothing','Planning.'),q('How long may the community presentation be?','No more than 10 minutes','30 minutes','2 minutes','One hour','Same as Round Two.'),q('What is health literacy?','The ability to find, understand and use health information','The number of health books you own','Knowing medical Latin','Reading speed','Plain language helps.'),q('Which is a good way to measure impact?','A pre/post survey','Counting slides','Guessing','Asking only team members','Learning gains.'),q('Which is plain language?','“Check who wrote it before you share”','“Assess epistemic provenance”','“Utilize heuristics”','“Leverage synergies”','Clear wording.'),q('What must be stated before Round Two begins?','The date and audience of the community presentation','The team’s grades','The judges’ names','The trailer length','Verification.'),q('Which is an official topic reference?','WHO guidance on combatting misinformation online','A random blog','An influencer video','A tabloid','Event resources.'),q('Why fact-check every statistic you use?','To build trust and model the skills you teach','To make the presentation longer','It is not necessary','To confuse the audience','Practice what you preach.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how your team would plan its community presentation on health misinformation, including audience, partner, objectives, plain language and impact measurement.',rubric:[['Audience',['target audience','why','platforms','myths','tailor','language']],['Partner and logistics',['library','school','senior center','partner','date','10 minutes','permission','record']],['Objectives and literacy',['objectives','sift','red flags','plain language','health literacy','visuals']],['Impact and research',['pre','post','survey','pledge','feedback','sources','cite','hhs','who']]],model:'Our target audience would be the parent group at our district’s middle school, because parents make health decisions for their children, often see health claims in social media and messaging apps and asked our principal for help managing what their kids see online. We would contact the PTA president, explain HOSA and the topic, and book a 10-minute slot at the March meeting, confirming the room, a projector or easel and permission to record parts of the presentation for our trailer. We would note the date and audience so we can state them before Round Two. Our learning objectives are that parents will be able to name the four SIFT steps, identify three red flags of health misinformation and name two trustworthy sources such as their child’s pediatrician and the health department. We would use plain language—short sentences, common words and defined terms—along with visuals, an invented example post to fact-check together and handouts in English and Spanish. To measure impact, we would give a three-question anonymous pre- and post-survey with a QR code, offer “I check before I share” pledge cards and ask the PTA president for feedback. All statistics would come from the official references—HHS, Johns Hopkins and WHO—and peer-reviewed research, cited on our poster and handout.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_HL,CDC_PLAIN]};

export const PH7={title:'Designing a captivating ten-minute presentation',hook:'“Anything goes” in Public Health—skits, games, costumes, music, props and live polls are all allowed. That freedom is a gift and a challenge. The best presentations use creativity to make one clear message unforgettable, keep the audience engaged for all ten minutes and end with a call to action people will actually follow.',objectives:['Structure a ten-minute presentation with a strong opening and closing.','Use interactive and creative techniques to captivate an audience.','Choose visual aids, props and costumes that add value.','Build flow and transitions so every member contributes.'],sections:[
['A ten-minute structure',[
'A clear structure keeps the presentation focused. **Opening (0:00–1:00):** grab attention and state the purpose. **Why it matters (1:00–2:30):** define health misinformation and show its real-world harm. **How to spot it (2:30–5:00):** red flags and the SIFT method, demonstrated live. **Myth busting (5:00–7:30):** two or three myths relevant to your audience, corrected with truth sandwiches. **What you can do (7:30–9:00):** practice and pledge. **Closing (9:00–10:00):** memorable statement with impact.',
'Rehearse to finish at about **9:30–9:45**. In Round Two, a timecard shows **one minute remaining** and the presentation is **stopped at 10 minutes**. Anything after that is not scored.',
'Every section should support **one core message**, such as “Pause, check, then share.” Repeat it so the audience remembers it.',
order('Ten-minute flow',['Opening and purpose','Why misinformation matters','How to spot it: red flags and SIFT','Myth busting with truth sandwiches','Audience practice and pledge','Memorable closing with impact'])]],
['Openings and closings',[
'The rating sheet’s **opening** criterion rewards teams that **establish the occasion and purpose**, **grab attention** and make the audience want to listen. Try a skit of someone scrolling and believing a fake post, a surprising statistic, a quick “real or fake?” poll or a question: “How many of you have seen a health claim this week that seemed too good to be true?”',
'The **closing** criterion rewards teams that **prepare the audience for the ending** and **end memorably**, including **impact on the community** and **feedback from the presentation**. In Round Two, you can share what happened when you presented to your community: “At the library, 92% of parents could name the SIFT steps after our talk, up from 15%.” End with your core message and call to action.',
'Write and memorize your first and last lines. They frame everything in between.',
sort('Strong or weak opening?',['Strong','Weak'],[['A 20-second skit of a viral fake post',0],['“Hi, our project is about misinformation”',1],['A live “real or fake?” poll',0],['Reading a definition off a poster',1],['A surprising, sourced statistic',0]])]],
['Captivating techniques',[
'The **captivating** criterion rewards teams that **actively engage the audience** and **keep attention** throughout. In community presentations, use **interactive** elements: hand-raise polls, a “fact or fiction” game with cards, a live fact-check of an invented post, a role-play of correcting a friend, or a quiz with small prizes. Change speakers and activities every one to two minutes.',
'In **Round Two**, you may pose **rhetorical questions** to judges but may **not** engage in **direct dialogue** with them or give them **materials**. Adapt interactive moments: show a recording of your community audience playing the game, or act out the interaction among team members.',
'Use **stories** and **characters**—a fictional teen named “Scrolling Sam” who learns to fact-check—to make information memorable.',
match('Technique and example',[['Interactive poll','“Raise your hand if you’ve seen this claim”'],['Game','Fact-or-fiction card game'],['Live demonstration','Fact-checking an invented post with SIFT'],['Skit','Correcting a friend respectfully'],['Story character','“Scrolling Sam” learns to check sources'],['Round Two adaptation','Rhetorical questions, no dialogue with judges']])]],
['Visual aids, props and costumes',[
'**Visual aids and presentation materials** are worth **10 points**. They should **add value and relevance**, help **tell a story** and offer **better understanding**—not replace speaking. Ideas: a giant phone “feed” poster with fake and real posts, a magnifying-glass prop for “investigate,” a stop sign for SIFT, costumes for a news-anchor skit or a slideshow on a battery-powered laptop.',
'Make visuals **large**, **readable** from across a room and consistent in color and font. Avoid clutter and long text. Label examples of false information clearly as **false** so no one leaves believing them.',
'For Round Two, remember there is **no electricity** and **no internet** unless you provide it; use battery-powered devices and bring backups. HOSA provides a **table**; everything else is yours to bring, set up in **three minutes** and tear down in **three minutes**.',
sort('Adds value or distracts?',['Adds value','Distracts'],[['A giant “feed” poster comparing real and fake posts',0],['A slide crammed with tiny text',1],['A stop-sign prop for the S in SIFT',0],['Costumes unrelated to the message',1],['Labeled “FALSE” stamp on myth examples',0]])]],
['Distinction, flow and team roles',[
'**Distinction** (10 points) rewards a highly creative, original and imaginative presentation that **stands out**. Consider a theme—a game show (“Fact or Cap?”), a detective agency (“The Myth Busters Bureau”), a news broadcast or a courtroom where a myth is “on trial.” A theme makes your presentation memorable and gives structure to creative elements.',
'**Flow, logic and transitions** (5 points) show evidence of practice. Plan smooth handoffs (“Now our detective, Jordan, will investigate the source”). Every member must take an **active speaking role**; divide sections so speaking time is balanced.',
'Rehearse with props, costumes and timing until transitions are seamless. A practiced team looks confident and keeps the audience’s attention.',
chk('Which idea best shows distinction?',['A “Myth Busters Bureau” detective theme where each member investigates a myth with SIFT','Reading facts from a poster','Copying a video from the internet','Showing one slide for ten minutes'],'A creative theme makes the message memorable.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Improving engagement',{a:{text:'In rehearsal, your team lectures for ten minutes from posters. (Scenario invented for practice.) What would most improve the captivating score?',choices:[['Add a live fact-check demo, a fact-or-fiction game and speaker changes every one to two minutes','b','Correct.',true],['Speak faster','b','Engagement matters more than speed.',false]]},b:{text:'For Round Two, your community version included audience members playing the game. How do you adapt it?',choices:[['Show a short recording of the community game or act it out among team members, using rhetorical questions for judges','c','Correct.',true],['Hand the judges game cards','c','You may not give judges materials.',false]]},c:{text:'Your rehearsals now finish at 9:40 with every member speaking and a memorable close.',end:true}})]],
["Sample scripts for key moments",["Scripting key moments helps your team deliver them smoothly. **Opening line:** “Every day, you scroll past hundreds of posts. Some of them could hurt your health. Today, we’ll show you how to spot the fakes before they spread.” **Transition:** “Now that you know why myths spread, let’s learn how to catch them—starting with one simple word: Stop.”","**Truth sandwich segment:** “Sunscreen protects your skin from cancer-causing UV rays. You may have heard that sunscreen itself causes cancer. That claim twists early, limited studies and ignores decades of research. Dermatologists agree: wear sunscreen every day.”","**Closing line:** “Before you share, take ten seconds to pause and check. That small habit can stop a myth from reaching the next person—and the next. Pause. Check. Then share.” Keep scripts conversational so they sound natural, not memorized.",{"t":"check","q":"Which transition best connects two sections?","options":["“Now that you know why myths spread, let’s learn how to catch them.”","“Okay, next.”","“Um, so…”","Silence"],"why":"A clear transition links ideas and supports flow scores."}]]
],takeaways:['Structure ten minutes around one core message: opening, why it matters, how to spot it (red flags, SIFT), myth busting, audience practice and pledge, closing; finish around 9:30–9:45.','Openings establish occasion and purpose and grab attention; closings end memorably with community impact and feedback; captivate with polls, games, live demos, skits and stories—in Round Two use rhetorical questions only, no dialogue or materials for judges.','Visuals, props and costumes (10 points) must add value and be readable, with myths labeled false; use battery power (no electricity or internet provided), set up in 3 minutes; a creative theme earns distinction; every member speaks with smooth transitions.'],terms:[['Core message','The one main idea the audience should remember.'],['Call to action','A specific action the audience is asked to take.'],['Distinction','A highly creative, original approach that stands out.']],mcqs:[q('What happens at 10 minutes in Round Two?','The presentation is stopped','Teams get extra time','Judges ask questions','Nothing','Timecard at one minute remaining.'),q('Which is allowed with judges in Round Two?','Rhetorical questions','Handing them materials','Direct dialogue','Asking them to play a game','No interaction.'),q('How much time do teams have to set up for Round Two?','Three minutes','Ten minutes','One minute','Fifteen minutes','And three to tear down.'),q('What does HOSA provide for Round Two?','A table','Electricity','Internet','A projector','Bring everything else.'),q('Visual aids should:','Add value and help tell the story','Replace speaking','Contain lots of tiny text','Be unrelated to the topic','10 points.'),q('Which closing element does the rating sheet reward?','Impact on the community and feedback','A new topic','Thanking only the team','Ending abruptly','Memorable close.'),q('Which technique best captivates a community audience?','A live fact-or-fiction game','Reading a script','Long silent pauses','Turning away from the audience','Interactive.'),q('Who must speak in the presentation?','Every team member','Only the captain','Only two members','No one; use a video','Active speaking role.')],frq:{prompt:'Design a creative ten-minute community presentation on health misinformation, describing its theme, structure, interactive elements, visual aids and closing.',rubric:[['Theme and structure',['theme','opening','why','sift','myth','pledge','closing','10 minutes']],['Engagement',['interactive','poll','game','demo','skit','story','speakers']],['Materials',['props','poster','costume','visual','readable','battery','false label']],['Closing and impact',['memorable','core message','call to action','impact','feedback','survey']]],model:'Our presentation is themed “The Myth Busters Bureau,” with each team member dressed as a detective. We open with a 30-second skit in which a parent scrolls a giant cardboard phone and believes a fake post claiming a tea cures the flu, then our lead detective states the occasion and purpose: helping families stop health myths before they spread. From 1:00 to 2:30, we define health misinformation, explain WHO’s term “infodemic” and share a sourced example of harm, like measles outbreaks after vaccination rates fall. From 2:30 to 5:00, we teach red flags and demonstrate SIFT live on an invented post, holding up a stop sign, a magnifying glass, a “better coverage” newspaper and a “trace” map. From 5:00 to 7:30, we bust three myths relevant to parents—detox teas, sunscreen and the MMR–autism myth—using truth sandwiches, with each myth stamped “FALSE” on our poster. From 7:30 to 9:00, the audience plays a fact-or-fiction card game and signs “Pause, Check, Then Share” pledge cards. We close by repeating our core message and, in Round Two, sharing our impact: at the community presentation, the share of parents who could name all four SIFT steps rose from 15% to 92%, and the PTA asked us to return next year. Our visuals are large, readable and battery-powered, every member speaks and we finish at about 9:40.'},sources:[RULES,WHO_ONLINE,SG_MIS]};

export const PH8={title:'The three-minute trailer video and display time',hook:'Think of the last movie trailer that made you want to see a film. In about two minutes it showed the stakes, the story and just enough of the best moments to leave you wanting more. Your Round One video is a trailer for your community presentation, and it decides whether you reach Round Two—so it needs to “wow.”',objectives:['Explain the Round One rating sheet and the three-minute limit.','Plan and storyboard a compelling trailer.','Film and edit with good focus, audio and transitions.','Upload a public link correctly and prepare for the required display time.'],sections:[
['What the trailer must do',[
'Round One gives each team **three minutes** of video to **convince judges** of the power and effectiveness of the community presentation. It is pre-judged virtually and worth **90 points**: **length** no more than three minutes (10, all-or-nothing), **video quality**—focus, audio and editing (10), **appropriate to the annual topic** (10), **effectiveness** (15), **impact** with a positive call to action (15), **creativity and originality** (15) and **leaves judges wanting to know more** (15).',
'The trailer does **not** have to be shown to the community, and **actors** may be used—team members or not. It can include clips from your community presentation, but it should be **edited** like a trailer, not a recording of the whole talk.',
'The goal is a judge saying, “When are you presenting Round Two?!”',
match('Rating-sheet item and points',[['Length (≤3 minutes)','10'],['Video quality','10'],['Appropriate to the annual topic','10'],['Effectiveness','15'],['Impact and call to action','15'],['Leaves judges wanting more','15']])]],
['Storyboarding a trailer',[
'Plan before filming. A trailer structure: **Hook (0:00–0:15)**—a striking moment, like a phone buzzing with a fake “miracle cure.” **Stakes (0:15–0:45)**—why misinformation matters, with a sourced fact. **Story (0:45–2:00)**—highlights of your presentation: the theme, a myth being busted, the audience playing your game. **Impact (2:00–2:40)**—results and audience reactions. **Call to action and tease (2:40–3:00)**—your core message and a line that leaves judges wanting more.',
'Sketch a **storyboard**: one box per shot with notes on visuals, dialogue, music and text. Make sure the **annual topic** is clear early—say or show “Fact-Check Your Feed” within the first 30 seconds.',
'Keep it **under three minutes**, including titles and credits. A video that runs even a few seconds over loses all 10 length points.',
order('Trailer storyboard',['Hook that grabs attention','Stakes: why misinformation matters','Story: highlights of the presentation','Impact: results and reactions','Call to action and tease','Short credits within 3:00'])]],
['Filming and editing for quality',[
'The **quality** item rewards **sharp images**, clear **sound** and **editing** with transitions that help the message stand out. Film in good light, keep the camera steady (use a tripod), record **horizontal** video for widescreen viewing unless your concept needs vertical, and use an external microphone or quiet rooms. Watch for background noise.',
'Edit tightly: cut pauses, vary shots (wide, medium, close-up), use **captions** for key lines and on-screen **text** for statistics with sources. Add music that fits the mood, keeping it below voices, and use music you have the **right to use** (royalty-free or licensed).',
'Watch the final cut on a phone and a laptop, with and without sound, and ask someone outside the team for feedback.',
"Export at a standard resolution (such as 1080p) and check that captions and on-screen text are readable on a small screen.",
sort('Helps or hurts video quality?',['Helps','Hurts'],[['Tripod and good lighting',0],['Loud music over dialogue',1],['Captions for key lines',0],['Blurry, shaky footage',1],['Varied shots and clean cuts',0],['Background noise from a hallway',1]])]],
['Effectiveness, impact and creativity',[
'**Effectiveness** rewards a video that **captivates**, delivers a **clear message**, **evokes emotion** and shows the topic’s importance. Real stakes—such as a family worried about a viral claim—build emotion. **Impact** rewards a **positive call to action**, such as “Pause, check, then share.” Avoid fear-only messaging.',
'**Creativity and originality** reward clever, original concepts: a mock movie trailer (“This spring… one feed… one myth… one team”), a news broadcast, a detective story or a “glitching” social feed effect. Use original content you created; credit any sources.',
'**Leaving judges wanting more** comes from **teasing** your full presentation: show your best moments but not everything, end on a question or cliffhanger and display your theme’s name prominently.',
chk('Which ending best leaves judges wanting more?',['A cliffhanger tease of the full presentation with the call to action','A full recap of everything in the presentation','A long list of credits','No ending at all'],'Show the best moments and tease the rest.')]],
['Uploading and display time',[
'One team member uploads the **link** to the trailer through the HOSA Digital Upload System by **May 15**. The link must be **public**—judges **will not request access**, and inaccessible links earn **no points**. Test it in a private browser window on several devices. If you used the upload system at your state conference, you must **upload again** for ILC.',
'At ILC, all teams must attend the **Required Project Display Time**; **missing it costs 15 points** from Round Two. Bring a **laptop, tablet or other battery-powered device** to play the trailer; you will have **15 minutes to set up**, **no electricity** and **no internet** unless you provide it—so download the video to the device.',
'Round One scoring happens in early June, but results are not released until after display time. Treat display time professionally: be present, dressed appropriately and ready to talk about your project.',
order('Upload and display checklist',['Export the final video under 3:00','Upload to a platform and set it to public','Test the link in a private window','Submit the link by May 15','Download the video to a charged device','Attend display time with the device'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Trailer review',{a:{text:'Your trailer runs 3:12. (Scenario invented for practice.) What must you do?',choices:[['Cut it to 3:00 or less','b','Correct. Over three minutes loses all length points.',true],['Leave it; a few seconds will not matter','b','The length item is all-or-nothing.',false]]},b:{text:'A reviewer says the topic is not clear until the end. How do you fix it?',choices:[['State or show “Fact-Check Your Feed” within the first 30 seconds','c','Correct.',true],['Add more music','c','Make the topic clear early.',false]]},c:{text:'The final 2:55 trailer opens with a hook, shows the topic early, highlights your myth-busting game and ends with a tease. You upload the public link on May 8.',end:true}})]],
["Legal and ethical media use",["Use only content you have the right to use. **Music** should be royalty-free or licensed for your use; popular songs can trigger copyright blocks that make your video unavailable to judges. **Images and clips** should be your own or properly licensed and credited. Avoid using real people’s misinformation posts with names visible; recreate invented examples instead.","Get **written permission** from anyone who appears on camera, including actors and community audience members, following your school’s policy for minors. Do not show identifying details of private individuals.","If you include news clips or statistics, cite the source on screen. Ethical media use protects your eligibility, your partners and the people you film.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Appropriate for the trailer?","buckets":["Appropriate","Not appropriate"],"items":[["Royalty-free background music",0],["A popular song without permission",1],["An invented example post",0],["A screenshot of a real person’s post with their name",1],["Footage of consenting audience members",0],["A news clip with no credit",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Round One is a pre-judged trailer of no more than 3 minutes (90 points): length (10, all-or-nothing), quality (10), annual topic (10), effectiveness (15), impact and call to action (15), creativity (15), leaves judges wanting more (15); actors may be used.','Storyboard a hook, stakes, story highlights, impact and a call-to-action tease; show the topic early; film with good light, steady shots and clear audio; edit tightly with captions and licensed music.','Upload a public link by May 15 (judges will not request access); attend the Required Display Time at ILC with a charged, battery-powered device and downloaded video (no electricity or internet), or lose 15 points.'],terms:[['Trailer','A short video that previews a longer work to make viewers want more.'],['Storyboard','A shot-by-shot plan for a video.'],['Display time','A required ILC session where teams show their trailer.']],mcqs:[q('What is the maximum length of the trailer?','3 minutes','5 minutes','10 minutes','1 minute','All-or-nothing points.'),q('How many points is Round One worth?','90','115','50','200','Pre-judged.'),q('What happens if judges cannot access the link?','No points are awarded','Judges request access','The team gets extra time','Nothing','Must be public.'),q('What is the penalty for missing the Required Display Time?','15 points','5 points','Disqualification','No penalty','From Round Two.'),q('Is electricity provided at display time?','No','Yes','Only for laptops','Only on request','Bring battery power.'),q('Must the trailer be shown to the community?','No','Yes','Only at a school','Only to parents','It is for judges.'),q('Which helps video quality?','A tripod and good lighting','Shaky handheld footage','Background noise','Music louder than voices','Sharp and clear.'),q('When should the annual topic appear in the trailer?','Early, within the first 30 seconds','Only in the credits','Never','In the last second','Make it clear.')],frq:{prompt:'Plan a three-minute Round One trailer for your community presentation on health misinformation, including its storyboard, quality choices and how you will upload and display it.',rubric:[['Storyboard',['hook','stakes','story','impact','call to action','tease','3 minutes']],['Topic and creativity',['fact-check your feed','theme','creative','original','emotion']],['Quality',['lighting','tripod','audio','captions','editing','music','licensed']],['Upload and display',['public link','may 15','private browser','display time','battery','download','15 points']]],model:'Our trailer will be a mock movie preview for “The Myth Busters Bureau.” From 0:00 to 0:15, the hook shows a phone buzzing in the dark with a post reading “Miracle tea cures the flu!” and a narrator whispering, “It started with one share.” From 0:15 to 0:45, we show the stakes: the title “Fact-Check Your Feed” appears with a sourced fact about WHO’s “infodemic,” and a worried parent stares at the screen. From 0:45 to 2:00, quick cuts from our library presentation show detectives demonstrating SIFT with a stop-sign prop, a myth being stamped “FALSE” and parents playing our fact-or-fiction game. From 2:00 to 2:40, we show impact: on-screen text shows that the share of parents naming all four SIFT steps rose from 15% to 92%, with a short partner quote. From 2:40 to 2:55, our core message—“Pause. Check. Then share.”—appears, followed by a tease: “The case isn’t closed. See the full investigation in Round Two.” We will film with a tripod and good lighting, use a clip-on microphone, add captions and royalty-free music below the voices and keep the final cut under three minutes. One member will upload the video, set the link to public, test it in a private browser on two devices and submit it by early May. For ILC display time, we will download the video to a fully charged tablet and attend in business attire to avoid the 15-point deduction.'},sources:[RULES,UPLOADS]};

export const PH9={title:'Round Two: logistics and scoring',hook:'Round Two is where everything comes together: your research, your community experience, your creativity and your teamwork. Judges score 115 points across content, organization, materials and delivery. Knowing the logistics and the rubric in detail lets your team prepare for every point—and avoid losing any to preventable mistakes.',objectives:['Follow the Round Two rules for setup, timing, equipment and judge interaction.','Explain each content criterion and how to earn top scores.','Plan the community confirmation statement.','Prepare backups for technology and props.'],sections:[
['Round Two rules',[
'Teams have **three minutes to set up** and **three minutes to tear down**. Before starting, the team states the **date and audience** of the community presentation; **time starts after** this statement, and if it is not shared within **30 seconds**, the timer announces that time has started. The full presentation is **no more than 10 minutes**, with a timecard at **one minute remaining**.',
'There is **no electricity** and **no internet** provided; battery-powered devices and your own hotspot are allowed. HOSA provides **a table**. **Notecards** are allowed; electronic notes must be on a different device from any device shown to judges. Teams may ask **rhetorical questions** but may **not** engage judges in dialogue or give them materials **before, during or after** the presentation. **All members must speak.**',
'The presentation should be the **same** one performed in the community. Small refinements are fine, but judges expect to see what your audience saw.',
flip('Round Two rules',[['Setup','3 minutes'],['Tear down','3 minutes'],['Presentation length','Up to 10 minutes'],['Before starting','State the date and audience'],['Power and internet','Not provided; batteries allowed'],['Judges','Rhetorical questions only; no materials']])]],
['The community confirmation statement',[
'The first content item—**community presentation confirmed** (10 points)—is all-or-nothing: you earn it by stating the **date and audience** before the presentation. Use a clear sentence: “The following presentation was completed for 45 parents at the Lincoln Middle School PTA meeting on March 3, 2027.”',
'Practice this statement so it is smooth and confident. Have one member deliver it as setup ends, then begin your opening immediately. If you presented to more than one audience, choose the main one or mention both briefly.',
'Keep evidence of the community presentation—photos, a partner email or a flyer—in case your state or region asks for verification.',
chk('What earns the 10-point community confirmation item?',['Stating the date and audience of the community presentation before starting','Showing a photo to the judges','Handing judges a flyer','Mentioning it at the end'],'State it before the timer starts.')]],
['Content criteria in depth',[
'Beyond confirmation, six content items are worth **10 points each**. **Importance of information** rewards a compelling interpretation of the topic that reinforces this year’s theme. **Overall understanding** rewards clearly and accurately sharing the **complexity** of the issue—for example, explaining both individual habits and platform and societal factors. **Effectiveness/impact** rewards clear evidence that the presentation educated the public and made a **positive impact**—share your survey results.',
'**Captivating** rewards active engagement throughout. **Distinction** rewards a highly creative, original and imaginative presentation with a “wow” factor. **Research/resources** rewards significant, reliable research—cite sources visibly and use accurate statistics.',
'Map each criterion to a specific moment in your presentation so nothing is left to chance.',
"For overall understanding, show that misinformation is complex: individual psychology, platform design, profit motives and gaps in access to trusted care all play a role. Teams that acknowledge this complexity while still offering practical solutions show the depth judges reward.",
match('Criterion and how to show it',[['Importance of information','Real harms of misinformation tied to the theme'],['Overall understanding','Psychology, platforms and solutions explained accurately'],['Effectiveness/impact','Pre/post survey results from the community'],['Captivating','Games, skits and speaker changes'],['Distinction','A creative theme like a detective bureau'],['Research/resources','Visible citations from HHS, WHO and studies']])]],
['Organization, materials and delivery',[
'**Organization** (15 points) includes **flow, logic and transitions**, the **opening** and the **closing** (5 each). **Presentation materials** (10 points) reward visual aids, props and costumes that **add value** and **tell a story**. **Delivery** (20 points) includes **voice**, **stage presence**, **diction, pronunciation and grammar** and **team participation** (5 each).',
'Together, content (70), organization (15), materials (10) and delivery (20) total **115 points**. Added to the Round One trailer (90), the maximum is **205**, minus any display-time penalty. Ties are broken by the highest-value rating-sheet sections.',
'Because delivery and organization total 35 points, rehearsal matters as much as content.',
{t:'table',caption:'Round Two scoring summary',head:['Section','Items','Points'],rows:[['Content','Community confirmed, importance, understanding, effectiveness, captivating, distinction, research','70'],['Organization','Flow and transitions, opening, closing','15'],['Materials','Visual aids, props, costumes','10'],['Delivery','Voice, stage presence, diction, team participation','20'],['Total','','115']]}]],
['Backups and setup plans',[
'Technology fails. Bring **fully charged** devices, **spare batteries** or power banks, downloaded videos (no internet needed), and a **printed backup** of key visuals. If a video will not play, have a teammate describe it in one sentence and continue.',
'Practice a **three-minute setup**: assign each member a task (poster stand, props, laptop, costumes), pack items in the order you will use them and rehearse setup with a timer. Practice **tear down** too, so you leave the room on time and professionally.',
'Bring tape, clips and an extra stand for posters. A calm, organized setup makes a strong first impression before you say a word.',
order('Three-minute setup plan',['Enter and greet judges briefly','Place poster stand and props on the table','Start the battery-powered device','Members take positions in costume','One member states the date and audience','Begin the opening'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Round Two morning',{a:{text:'Your laptop battery dies during setup. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Switch to your printed backup visuals and continue without delay','b','Correct.',true],['Ask the judges for an outlet','b','Electricity is not provided.',false]]},b:{text:'A judge looks confused during your game segment. Can you ask them a question and wait for an answer?',choices:[['No—use a rhetorical question and continue','c','Correct. No direct dialogue.',true],['Yes—ask them to participate','c','Judges may not be engaged in dialogue.',false]]},c:{text:'You finish at 9:45, tear down in three minutes and leave the room tidy.',end:true}})]],
["Rehearsing like it is Round Two",["Hold at least two **full dress rehearsals** in a room similar to the competition: a table, no outlets, a three-minute setup timer and someone holding a one-minute card at nine minutes. Practice the confirmation statement, the full presentation and the three-minute tear down.","Invite teachers or other HOSA members to act as judges using the actual rating sheet. Ask them to score honestly and identify the lowest-scoring items. Focus your final practices on those items—often transitions, the closing or balanced speaking parts.","Prepare for surprises: a prop breaks, a member is sick or the room is smaller than expected. Decide in advance who covers each role and how you will adapt.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Dress rehearsal plan","steps":["Set up a room with a table and no outlets","Time a three-minute setup","Deliver the confirmation statement","Run the full presentation with a one-minute card","Tear down in three minutes","Review rating-sheet feedback and fix the weakest items"]}]]
],takeaways:['Round Two: 3 minutes setup and tear down; state the date and audience first (time starts after; 30-second limit); up to 10 minutes with a one-minute card; no electricity or internet; HOSA provides a table; rhetorical questions only, no dialogue or materials for judges; all members speak.','Content (70): community confirmed (10, all-or-nothing), importance, understanding of complexity, effectiveness/impact, captivating, distinction and research (10 each); organization (15), materials (10) and delivery (20) bring the total to 115.','Round One (90) plus Round Two (115) = 205 maximum, minus any display penalty; bring charged devices, power banks, downloaded videos and printed backups; rehearse a three-minute setup and tear down.'],terms:[['Community confirmation','Stating the date and audience of the community presentation before Round Two.'],['Rhetorical question','A question asked for effect, without expecting an answer.'],['Backup plan','A prepared alternative if technology or props fail.']],mcqs:[q('How long is setup for Round Two?','3 minutes','10 minutes','1 minute','30 minutes','Plus 3 minutes tear down.'),q('When does the presentation time start?','After the team states the date and audience','When the team enters','After the judges speak','At setup','Within 30 seconds.'),q('How many points is Round Two worth?','115','90','70','205','Content, organization, materials, delivery.'),q('What is the maximum combined score?','205','115','90','300','Before penalties.'),q('Which is NOT allowed?','Giving judges a handout','Rhetorical questions','Battery-powered laptops','Notecards','No materials for judges.'),q('How many points is the community confirmation item?','10','5','15','20','All-or-nothing.'),q('Which content item rewards sharing the complexity of the issue?','Overall understanding','Captivating','Distinction','Community confirmed','Accurate depth.'),q('If a video will not play, you should:','Describe it briefly and continue','Stop the presentation','Ask judges for help','Start over','Stay on time.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your team would prepare for Round Two to earn as many of the 115 points as possible, including the rules you must follow.',rubric:[['Rules',['3 minutes','setup','tear down','date and audience','10 minutes','no electricity','table','rhetorical','no materials','all members']],['Content',['community confirmed','importance','understanding','complexity','impact','survey','captivating','distinction','research']],['Organization and materials',['flow','transitions','opening','closing','visual aids','props','costumes']],['Delivery and backups',['voice','stage presence','diction','team participation','backup','battery','rehearse']]],model:'We would rehearse a three-minute setup in which each member has a task—poster stand, props, battery-powered laptop and costumes—and as setup ends, one member would say, “The following presentation was completed for 45 parents at the Lincoln Middle School PTA meeting on March 3, 2027,” earning the 10-point community confirmation before time starts. We would present the same 10-minute presentation we gave in the community, finishing around 9:45 after the one-minute card. For content, we would explain the importance of the topic with real, sourced harms of misinformation; show our overall understanding by covering the psychology of sharing, platform algorithms and practical solutions like SIFT; prove effectiveness and impact with our pre/post survey results; captivate with our detective skit, live fact-check and fact-or-fiction game, adapted for judges with rhetorical questions only; earn distinction with our “Myth Busters Bureau” theme; and display citations from HHS, WHO and peer-reviewed studies for research. For organization, we would practice transitions, a strong opening skit and a closing with community impact and feedback. Our props, poster and costumes would add meaning, not clutter. For delivery, every member would speak with clear volume, varied pace, eye contact and no fillers. We would bring charged devices, a power bank, a downloaded video and printed backup visuals, never give judges materials and tear down in three minutes.'},sources:[RULES,USPHS]};

export const PH10={title:'Delivery, ethics and careers in public health',hook:'A brilliant script delivered in a monotone loses its power; a simple message delivered with energy and sincerity can change minds. Public health communicators also face ethical questions: how to correct misinformation without shaming, how to use stories responsibly and how to stay accurate as science evolves. This lesson polishes your delivery and connects the event to real public health careers.',objectives:['Apply the four delivery criteria and rehearse effectively.','Communicate about misinformation ethically and respectfully.','Reflect on impact and plan next steps beyond the competition.','Explore careers in public health and health communication.'],sections:[
['Delivery that persuades',[
'**Voice** (5 points): speak loudly enough for the back of the room, vary rate and volume and use pauses—especially before key facts. **Stage presence** (5): stand tall, make eye contact, use purposeful gestures and show enthusiasm that fits your theme. **Diction, pronunciation and grammar** (5): pronounce terms clearly (“disinformation,” “infodemic”), avoid fillers and match tone to message. **Team participation** (5): every member speaks and shares the load.',
'In character-based presentations, stay in character while still speaking clearly to the audience. Face the judges, not the poster. When a teammate speaks, look at them and react naturally.',
'Record rehearsals to catch fillers, rushing and moments when the energy drops, then rehearse those sections again.',
"Match your energy to your message. Skits and games can be lively, while stories about real harm—such as a family affected by a preventable illness—call for a slower, more serious tone. Changing your tone at the right moments keeps the audience engaged and shows sincerity.",
sort('Strong or weak delivery?',['Strong','Weak'],[['Pausing before a key statistic',0],['Reading from the poster with your back to judges',1],['Clear pronunciation of “infodemic”',0],['Monotone voice for ten minutes',1],['Every member speaking with energy',0],['Constant “um” and “like”',1]])]],
['Communicating ethically',[
'Correct misinformation **without shaming** people who believe it. Avoid mocking examples that could stereotype a group or culture. Focus on **tactics** and **evidence**, not on attacking individuals. When using real examples of misinformation, **label them clearly** as false, avoid spreading them further and do not reproduce harmful content in detail.',
'Be **honest about uncertainty**: science evolves, and guidance can change as evidence grows—that is a strength, not a weakness. Use **accurate statistics** with sources, avoid exaggeration and protect the **privacy** of audience members in photos and recordings.',
'Model what you teach: check your own claims, correct mistakes publicly if you make one and give credit to the sources and partners who helped you.',
sort('Ethical or not?',['Ethical','Not ethical'],[['Labeling myth examples clearly as false',0],['Mocking a cultural group’s beliefs',1],['Explaining that guidance can change with new evidence',0],['Exaggerating a statistic for drama',1],['Getting consent before filming audience members',0],['Posting a myth without correction for views',1]])]],
['Reflecting on impact and next steps',[
'Reflect as a team after your community presentation: What did the survey show? What questions did the audience ask? What surprised you? What would you change? Use these insights to strengthen your Round Two presentation and your closing statement about **impact** and **feedback**.',
'Plan **next steps** that extend your impact: present to more audiences (another grade, a senior center, a faith group), share an approved fact-checking guide with your school, create a “check before you share” campaign, train peers to lead mini-workshops or partner with your local health department on a misinformation response plan.',
'Sharing impact and next steps shows judges that your presentation was real public health work, not just a performance.',
"Keep a simple impact record for each presentation: date, location, audience size, survey results, partner feedback and requests to return. These records strengthen your Round Two closing, your trailer and future applications for scholarships or programs.",
order('Post-presentation reflection',['Review survey and feedback','List audience questions','Identify what worked','Identify what to change','Update the Round Two presentation','Plan next audiences and partners'])]],
['Careers in public health and health communication',[
'Public health offers many careers. **Health education specialists** and **community health workers** teach people about behaviors that promote wellness and connect them to services; the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics reports that health education specialists typically need a **bachelor’s degree**. **Health communication specialists** design campaigns and messages for health agencies, hospitals and nonprofits. **Epidemiologists** study disease patterns, **biostatisticians** analyze data and **environmental health specialists** protect communities from hazards.',
'Other paths include **public health nursing**, **health policy**, **infodemic management** (a growing field at WHO and health agencies) and service in the **USPHS Commissioned Corps**. Many public health professionals earn a **Master of Public Health (MPH)**.',
'Skills from this event—research, data literacy, plain-language communication, creativity, teamwork and advocacy—are exactly what these careers require.',
match('Career and focus',[['Health education specialist','Teaches healthy behaviors'],['Community health worker','Connects people to services'],['Health communication specialist','Designs health messages and campaigns'],['Epidemiologist','Studies disease patterns'],['Infodemic manager','Tracks and responds to health misinformation'],['USPHS officer','Uniformed public health service']])]],
['Being a fact-checker for life',[
'The habits you teach—**pause, check, then share**—are lifelong skills. Keep a short list of trusted sources on your phone (your health department, MedlinePlus, major medical organizations), use SIFT when something seems surprising and talk with friends and family respectfully when they share myths.',
'As future health professionals, you will be **trusted messengers** for patients and communities. People will ask you about things they saw online. Responding with **empathy, evidence and clear language** will make you more effective in any health career.',
'Your presentation can end with this idea: everyone has a role in stopping misinformation, and small actions—one pause, one check, one correction—add up to healthier communities.',
chk('Which habit best summarizes this year’s topic?',['Pause, check, then share','Share first, check later','Never use social media','Believe whatever is popular'],'Stop myths before they spread.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Final polish',{a:{text:'Practice judges say your content is strong, but two members barely speak and the team rushes the closing. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you change?',choices:[['Rebalance speaking parts and cut a minute elsewhere so the closing has time','b','Correct.',true],['Keep it; content is what matters','b','Delivery and closing are scored.',false]]},b:{text:'One skit mocks an older adult who shares a myth. Is that a problem?',choices:[['Yes—revise it to show respectful correction without stereotyping','c','Correct.',true],['No—it gets laughs','c','Avoid shaming and stereotypes.',false]]},c:{text:'Your revised skit shows a grandchild helping a grandparent use SIFT together, and your closing ends on time.',end:true}})]],
["Handling nerves and staying confident",["Nerves are normal and can even improve performance when channeled into energy. Before presenting, take slow breaths, do a quick team huddle and remind yourselves of your purpose: you already helped real people in your community.","If something goes wrong—a forgotten line, a stuck prop—keep going. Teammates can cover smoothly if everyone knows the overall flow. Judges remember how teams recover more than small mistakes.","Confidence comes from preparation. The more you rehearse, the more natural your delivery becomes, letting your enthusiasm for the topic shine through.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Helpful or unhelpful response to nerves?","buckets":["Helpful","Unhelpful"],"items":[["Slow breathing before entering",0],["Stopping the presentation after a mistake",1],["A teammate smoothly covering a forgotten line",0],["Skipping rehearsals to avoid stress",1],["Reminding yourselves of your community impact",0]]}]]
],takeaways:['Delivery (20 points): voice, stage presence, diction and team participation; face judges, pause before key facts, avoid fillers, keep energy in character and give every member a balanced role.','Communicate ethically: correct without shaming or stereotyping, label myths clearly, be honest that science evolves, use accurate sourced statistics, protect privacy and model good fact-checking.','Reflect on survey results and feedback to strengthen Round Two and plan next steps; public health careers include health education, community health work, health communication, epidemiology, infodemic management and the USPHS Commissioned Corps; pause, check, then share.'],terms:[['Health communication specialist','A professional who designs health messages and campaigns.'],['Trusted messenger','A person whose credibility helps others accept accurate information.'],['Master of Public Health (MPH)','A common graduate degree for public health careers.']],mcqs:[q('How many points is delivery worth?','20','10','70','115','Four criteria.'),q('Which shows strong stage presence?','Eye contact and purposeful gestures','Back to the judges','Fidgeting','Looking at the floor','Poise.'),q('How should myth examples be presented?','Clearly labeled as false','Without any label','As if true','In tiny print','Avoid spreading them.'),q('Why say that science evolves?','Guidance can change with new evidence, which is a strength','To show science is useless','To confuse the audience','It is not true','Honest uncertainty.'),q('Which career designs health messages and campaigns?','Health communication specialist','Surgeon','Radiologist','Pharmacist','Communication.'),q('Health education specialists typically need a:','Bachelor’s degree','High school diploma only','Medical degree','No education','BLS.'),q('Which is an ethical practice?','Getting consent before filming audience members','Exaggerating statistics','Mocking groups','Spreading myths for views','Privacy.'),q('Which summarizes the topic’s habit?','Pause, check, then share','Share first','Never check','Trust every post','Fact-check your feed.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your team will deliver its presentation effectively and ethically, and describe how the skills from this event connect to public health careers.',rubric:[['Delivery',['voice','volume','pause','eye contact','gestures','fillers','every member']],['Ethics',['shaming','stereotype','label','false','uncertainty','evolves','accurate','privacy']],['Reflection',['survey','feedback','impact','next steps','audiences','partners']],['Careers',['health education','community health worker','health communication','epidemiologist','usphs','mph','skills']]],model:'To deliver effectively, every member will have a balanced speaking role in our detective theme, and we will speak loudly enough for the back of the room, vary our pace and pause before key statistics, like our survey results. We will face the judges instead of our poster, make eye contact, use purposeful gestures and record rehearsals to remove fillers and practice terms like “infodemic” and “disinformation.” To communicate ethically, we will correct myths without shaming the people who believe them: our skit shows a grandchild helping a grandparent use SIFT together rather than mocking anyone, and we avoid examples that stereotype any group. Every myth example will be clearly labeled false, every statistic will be accurate and cited and we will explain that health guidance can change as evidence grows, which is how science works. We will also protect privacy by using only footage of audience members who consented. After our community presentation, we will review our survey and feedback to improve Round Two and plan next steps, such as presenting at the senior center and sharing a fact-checking guide with our school. These skills connect directly to public health careers: health education specialists and community health workers teach and connect people to services, health communication specialists design campaigns like ours, epidemiologists use the same data skills and infodemic managers track and respond to misinformation, and many professionals serve in the USPHS Commissioned Corps after earning an MPH.'},sources:[RULES,BLS_HE,APHA,USPHS]};
